# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.triangle).
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT IS FOR. RedwoodTriangle.app -- an application of this port's own, a plain
# bundle in /Applications that SpringBoard launches -- opens a window, makes a CAMetalLayer,
# takes a drawable from it, draws one triangle and presents it. Three things that no measurement
# so far could even ask are asked here, and they fail SEPARATELY, which is why the list below
# runs two different processes:
#
#   1  DOES THE DRIVER WORK OUTSIDE APPLE'S DAEMONS. Every Metal result this port has was
#      measured inside backboardd, SpringBoard, mediaserverd or a /redwood/bin diagnostic.
#      An application is a different process with a different launch path, a different user
#      (mobile) and UIKit above it, and nobody has ever tried one.
#   2  DOES THE DRAWABLE WORK. -[CAMetalLayer nextDrawable] asks a device exactly one thing,
#      -newTextureWithDescriptor:iosurface:plane: (docs/metal.md 10.46). That method landed in
#      src/redwoodmetal.m today and HAS NEVER EXECUTED. This boot is its first caller.
#   3  DOES PRESENTATION REACH THE SCREEN. Presentation goes through no device at all: the
#      drawable's IOSurface is registered in the layer's CAImageQueue by surface id and the
#      window server takes it from there. A triangle on the panel is that whole chain at once,
#      and it is the one thing a screenshot can prove and a log cannot.
#
# WHICH ROAD THE DEVICE COMES BY, AND WHY THIS LIST NOW WORKS IN BOTH BOOTS. There are two,
# and src/triangle_app.m asks Metal FIRST and only builds its own when Metal has none:
#
#   discovery ON    MetalPluginName on the IOAcceleratorES service (boot-arg metal=1 and
#                   /redwood/bin/metal_props on). MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice() answers our
#                   plug-in device and the application adds NOTHING. THIS IS THE ROAD A GAME
#                   TAKES, so it is the more interesting of the two and the one to prefer.
#   discovery OFF   MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice() answers nil and the application walks
#                   discovery's steps 3, 4 and 5 itself. Worth keeping: the only Metal device
#                   in the system is then the one it made, so every frame is attributable
#                   without an argument.
#
# THE ORDER IS NOT A PREFERENCE, IT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN RUNNING AND ABORTING, and the
# first edition of this file got it wrong and died for it in logs/055044-run.log -- the first
# boot that had a window server AND the registry switch, where the application was the only
# process in the whole system that aborted. MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice (0x181a34b3c) is a
# dispatch_sync of one block (0x181a3442c), and the block is three lines:
#
#     [devices count] == 0  ->  leave the answer nil and return
#     [devices count] != 1  ->  MTLReportFailure(..., 648, @"MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice
#                               doesn't know what to do with more than one device")  = abort()
#     otherwise             ->  [[devices objectAtIndex:0] retain]
#
# `devices` is the global at 0x1e1efe9a0 and MTLAddDevice (0x181a1875c) appends to that SAME
# array under the same queue (0x1e1efe9b0). So letting discovery register one and then
# registering one of our own makes two, and the next MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice() in that
# process dies on an assertion Apple wrote about a list it will not choose from.
#
# SO THE CORRECTION THAT MATTERS, because the opposite would have been a finding about this
# port: DISCOVERY DOES NOT FAIL IN A THIRD-PARTY APPLICATION. It succeeded. Apple's daemons did
# not abort in that boot for one reason only -- none of them calls MTLAddDevice; they let
# discovery register one device and ask for it, which is exactly what this application now
# does. A game is NOT in the position the first edition was in: a game never calls MTLAddDevice.
#
# AND THE WARNING THAT COMES WITH IT, for every list, not this one. The same sequence --
# probe, class, -initWithAcceleratorPort:, MTLAddDevice, probe -- is in
# userland/src/metal_drv_test.m (around :7340 and on), src/metal_dev.m and src/metal_stubdev.m.
# In a boot with the registry switch ON, every metal_drv_test mode that reaches it (--draw,
# --picture, --time, --draw-attrib, --draw-depth, --draw-index ...) will abort at the same
# instruction. Only --sweep is safe, because it returns at :7325 before that path, and
# metal_caps is safe because it takes the device from MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice(). DO NOT
# put metal_drv_test in a discovery-on boot's job list until it asks Metal first the way this
# application now does.
#
# WHAT IT NEEDS
#
#   the image          CRYPTEXES=flash SYSTEM=1 GL=1 QUIET=1 RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.triangle ./build.sh
#                      SYSTEM=1 IS REQUIRED and is the expensive part: the bundle goes into
#                      /Applications of the system image, and that image is rebuilt only when
#                      its own inputs are newer or when SYSTEM=1 says so. Without it the boot
#                      runs the old image, the application is not there, and only step 5 (the --layer line) says
#                      anything.
#                      GL=1 for /redwood/lib/libredwoodair.dylib -- no compiler library, no
#                      pipeline, and the driver says so by name. build.sh now hashes
#                      tools/mesa/air/ against $M/built-from-air and rebuilds it when they
#                      differ, so the stale-library trap of logs/235006-run.log (docs/metal.md
#                      10.47: driver rebuilt, library byte for byte the same) is closed; this
#                      is a double-check and not the only guard:
#                        nm -gU xnu-deps/build/mesa/out/libredwoodair.dylib | grep from_surface
#
#                      QUIET=1 IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE, AND logs/055044-run.log IS WHY: that boot
#                      had no QUIET, and the whole of this application's output is MISSING from
#                      it -- not because it failed to write, but because the console buffer is a
#                      RING and it wrapped. Two references that it wrapped rather than that we
#                      were silent: the stream is not in chronological order (lines stamped
#                      05:51 stand at line 64, 1321 lines from the single second 05:50:45 stand
#                      at line 1300, and 3199 more are from the last two minutes), and the JOB
#                      ECHOES stop after the third line too -- init writes those to the same
#                      /dev/console, so the loss is the buffer's and not this program's.
#                      build.sh says the cost in its own words: launchd "says a few thousand
#                      lines about its own business in the first minutes and pushes everything
#                      else out". It silences only launchd's accounting; every daemon keeps its
#                      standard error, and so does this application.
#                      JOBS is left at its default: those are the jobs SpringBoard needs to
#                      launch an application (lsd, runningboardd, containermanagerd, pkd are
#                      all in it). If the launch is refused, REST=1 is the thing to try next.
#   XL_CMDLINE         "bootcpu=7 cpus=8 cpufreq=1 touchscreen=1 gpu=1 metal=1 camtl=1
#                       metal_profile=1 mediamtl=1"
#                      -- the line of logs/055044-run.log, which is the first one that has the
#                      window server compositing AND Metal's discovery switched on.
#                      metal_profile=1 is what makes that possible: it claims Apple2, so
#                      MPSDevice::GetMPSDevice's ladder finds a yes and SpringBoard no longer
#                      aborts in the Siri orb (docs/metal.md 10.47). gpu=1 so the kernel has an
#                      Adreno and kRedwoodSurface can hand a surface's memory over as a GPU
#                      buffer; touchscreen=1 because the lock screen is swiped away below.
#                      NO -v, so the kernel's own console does not write on the panel over the
#                      picture.
#                      WITH metal=1 IN THE LINE, KEEP /redwood/bin/metal_props on IN STEP 1a
#                      BELOW: that is the discovery-on road, and it is the one worth measuring.
#                      Without metal=1 the metal_props line answers 0xe00002c7, the properties
#                      stay unset, and the application takes the other road and says so.
#   XL_SECS=420        the boot, a forty-second wait for SpringBoard, the launch, and then four
#                      minutes of the application repainting so that a screenshot can be taken
#                      at leisure.
#
#   AND NOT gpu_recover=1, which is the one boot-arg it would be tempting to add here. It is the
#   way out of the gpuWait latch that the --layer step below is ordered around (docs/air.md: a hang would
#   then cost one dispatch instead of the rest of the boot), and it HAS NEVER BEEN RUN -- its own
#   measurement, userland/rc.jobs.metalrecover, is still waiting for a boot. Putting an untried
#   kernel path into the boot that is meant to try the drawable would mean two new things at once
#   and no way to say which answer belonged to which.
#
#   AND TAKE THE SCREENSHOT while it is up, from another terminal:
#     ./xl screenshot logs/triangle.png
#   It may be taken more than once; the picture is identical every frame on purpose.
#
# THE PICTURE, WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE THE RUN. Three colours, and each means a different thing:
#
#   black        the layer's own backgroundColor. NOTHING WAS EVER PRESENTED.
#   pure blue    the render pass's clear colour, r g b = 0 0 255. A frame reached the screen
#                and the triangle did not cover that pixel.
#   orange       the triangle, r g b = 255 128 0.
#
# The panel must be: an ORANGE TRIANGLE filling the LOWER LEFT HALF of the screen, over PURE
# BLUE, with the straight edge running from the TOP LEFT corner to the BOTTOM RIGHT corner. No
# status bar (UIStatusBarHidden), no home indicator, nothing of iOS's own.
#
# Why that half and not another, so that the prediction is derived and not hoped for. The
# pipeline is asked for with NO vertex function, which makes the compiler library supply its own
# full-screen quad; that quad's four corners are in a fixed order, (-1,-1), (1,-1), (-1,1),
# (1,1) (rwa_quad_verts, tools/mesa/air/air_metal.c), so a draw of THREE vertices is the
# triangle through the first three. The viewport puts clip y = +1 in memory row 0
# (docs/metal.md 10.28), so clip (-1,+1) is the top left, (-1,-1) the bottom left and (1,-1)
# the bottom right -- and the interior is every pixel whose centre satisfies
#
#     (x + 0.5) / width  <  (y + 0.5) / height
#
# AND THE NUMBERS, which are exact rather than approximate. For the drawable this application
# gets (375 x 812 points at contentsScale 3 = 1125 x 2436):
#
#     2 740 500 pixels, of which 1 370 250 orange and 1 370 250 blue, 0 neither
#
# -- exactly half each, and that is a proof and not a coincidence: (x,y) -> (W-1-x, H-1-y) maps
# covered to uncovered one for one, and NO pixel centre lies on the edge, because
# (2x+1)*2436 is always even and (2y+1)*1125 is always odd and the two can never be equal.
# That is why a pixel which is NEITHER colour is a failure here and not a rounding; there is no
# multisampling and no filtering on this path, so every pixel is wholly in or wholly out.
#
# AND IF THE DRAWABLE COMES OUT ANOTHER SIZE, nothing above is invalidated and no number has to
# be recomputed by hand: the program counts the rule's own answer for whatever size it was given
# and prints it beside the measured one on the same line ("the rule says N orange and N blue").
# 1125 x 2436 is what 375 x 812 points at contentsScale 3 gives and what the log should say; a
# different number there is a finding about contentsScale, and the comparison still holds.
#
# WHAT EACH WAY OF BEING WRONG PUTS THERE INSTEAD. This table is what makes the photograph
# evidence; without it a picture that looks plausible would be read as a picture that is right.
#
#   the panel shows              what it means                                        where else it shows
#   ---------------------------  --------------------------------------------------   -------------------
#   orange triangle lower left   everything in questions 1, 2 and 3 works             "0 of 2740500" twice
#   over pure blue
#
#   BLACK, whole screen          nothing was ever presented. Either the application    "triangle: NOT
#                                never drew (no device, no pipeline, nextDrawable      MEASURED" or
#                                nil) or the present never reached the window          "frame N: nextDrawable
#                                server. The log tells the two apart by frame          gave NIL"
#                                number -- see the next row
#
#   the home screen, or the      the application did not come to the front: either     no "triangle: app:"
#   launch screen                SpringBoard refused the URL, or it launched and       lines at all, or
#                                UIKit never showed the window                         none after
#                                                                                      "didFinishLaunching"
#
#   pure blue, whole screen,     a frame DID reach the panel and the draw covered      "surface count: ...
#   no triangle                  nothing. The clear is the driver's and the draw is    2740500 are blue",
#                                not: a cull mode set where Metal's default is none,   wrong = 1370250
#                                or a draw of zero area
#
#   the triangle is the UPPER     the viewport's y sign has been lost. The covered      exactly 1370260 wrong,
#   LEFT half (edge from          half becomes (x+0.5)/W < 1 - (y+0.5)/H. This is a    first at (0,0), and
#   bottom left to top right)     finding about the viewport and NOT about the         (225,1948), (506,1339)
#                                 drawable -- and note that the sign is the one thing  and (112,2314) all turn
#                                 here measured only on this machine's softpipe and    BLUE while (618,1096)
#                                 never on the phone (rc.jobs.metalpicture: the bands  turns ORANGE
#                                 that would have shown it have never drawn)
#
#   the triangle is the UPPER     the picture is turned through 180 degrees: row 0 is  EVERY pixel wrong --
#   RIGHT half (edge still        scanned out last and column 0 last as well           2740500 of 2740500 --
#   corner to corner)                                                                   and all six named
#                                                                                       pixels inverted
#
#   AND THE TRAP IN BOTH OF THOSE ROWS, which is why the named pixels are not a luxury next to the
#   count: "of 2740500 pixels, N are orange, N are blue and 0 are NEITHER" reads EXACTLY THE SAME
#   -- 1370250 and 1370250 -- for the right picture, for the vertical flip and for the 180-degree
#   turn. The three orientations have the same areas. Only the line that counts pixels against the
#   RULE, the six named pixels and the photograph can tell them apart, and that is the whole reason
#   this file asks for three kinds of evidence instead of one.
#
#   BACKGROUND RED and the        red and blue are swapped: the attachment written as   if the swap is in the
#   triangle azure                RGBA8 where it is BGRA8. The two colours were         MEMORY, all 2740500
#                                 chosen to be each other's opposite in exactly those   pixels count as
#                                 two channels so that this is seen before it is        NEITHER in both views
#                                 counted -- a swapped orange is b g r 255 128 0 and    and (225,1948) reads
#                                 a swapped blue is 0 0 255, and NEITHER of those is    b g r 255 128 0. If
#                                 either of the two colours this program can produce    both views read 0
#                                                                                       wrong and only the
#                                                                                       PANEL is red, the
#                                                                                       swap is the window
#                                                                                       server's reading of
#                                                                                       the surface and not
#                                                                                       the draw
#
#   DIAGONAL STRIPES, or the      the row stride disagrees: the texture's pitch is not  "surface check" WRONG
#   edge sawtoothed, or the       the surface's own. The kernel gives 4544 bytes a row  and "getBytes check"
#   picture sheared               for 1125 BGRA pixels (1125 x 4 = 4500, aligned up to  AGREES -- precisely
#   progressively                 64 by MINISURF_ROW_ALIGN), and a texture laid out at  because -getBytes:
#                                 4500 shears by 11 pixels a row and wraps after a      goes through the
#                                 hundred. It should be impossible by construction --   TEXTURE'S layout and
#                                 fd_resource_from_handle sets layout.pitch0 to the     the other reads raw
#                                 stride it is handed, which is the kernel's number --  memory at the
#                                 so this row is about freedreno overriding it          KERNEL'S stride
#
#   the top of the screen is      an offset confusion: the pixels start `offset` bytes  the first wrong pixel
#   right and the bottom is       into the buffer and one side of the import did not    the log names is in
#   black or garbage, or the      account for it                                         row 0 or in the last
#   whole picture is shifted                                                             row, and the log says
#   down by a few rows                                                                   which
#
#   BLACK, or whatever was        THE TEXTURE WAS A COPY, not the surface -- the failure  "getBytes count" reads
#   on the layer before           rc.jobs.drawable's two-direction reading existed to     0 wrong and
#                                 catch. The GPU wrote somewhere else and the surface     "surface count" reads
#                                 the window server shows was never touched               2740500 NEITHER,
#                                                                                         because untouched
#                                                                                         surface memory is
#                                                                                         zeros and b g r
#                                                                                         0 0 0 is neither of
#                                                                                         this program's two
#                                                                                         colours
#
#   the triangle, correctly,       the processor's view of the surface is not coherent     "surface count"
#   WHILE "surface check"          with the GPU's write. NOT a failure of the drawable:    WRONG while
#   reads garbage                  the pixels did reach the surface -- that is what the    "getBytes count"
#                                  panel is showing -- and only this process's cached      reads 0 wrong
#                                  view of them is stale. IOSurfaceLock's return code is
#                                  printed on the line above it for exactly this case
#
#   HOW THOSE TWO ARE TOLD APART, since they give the SAME pair of log lines: by the panel, and
#   only by the panel. A copy leaves the screen without the triangle; a stale cached view leaves
#   it with one. This is the single place in this boot where the photograph and the log have to
#   be read AGAINST each other rather than each on its own -- which is why neither of them alone
#   was going to be enough, and why both are asked for.
#
# AND THE ONE OUTCOME THAT IS NOT A PICTURE: the log stopping. Every risky call says its own
# name first ("triangle: step: ..."), flushed, so a stall names the call it entered -- the
# picture mode of src/metal_drv_test.m cost one boot to learn that. The last "step:" line in the
# log is the suspect.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. IS THE BUNDLE EVEN IN THE IMAGE. Cheapest possible line and it comes first, because a
#    forgotten SYSTEM=1 makes everything below meaningless in a way that is otherwise hard to
#    tell apart from a refused launch. It must list FIVE entries -- RedwoodTriangle, Info.plist,
#    PkgInfo and the two icons -- all REAL FILES owned by root:wheel (vol_ls leaves out "." and
#    ".."). A symbolic link here is a bundle LaunchServices will refuse ("Unable to register");
#    nothing here at all means the system image is the old one, and then only the --layer step says anything.
/redwood/bin/vol_ls ls /Applications/RedwoodTriangle.app
#
# 1a. METAL'S OWN DISCOVERY, SWITCHED ON. Without this the registry has no MetalPluginName and
#     CoreAnimation keeps OpenGL ES: the boot of 22 October 01:39 had camtl=1 and still logged
#     "CoreAnimation's OpenGL ES renderer has no function 40", with not one line about a Metal
#     pipeline library, because a switch is not a request. The application does not need this --
#     it walks steps 3 to 5 itself -- so what this adds is the WINDOW SERVER on Metal beside it.
#
#     It was left out before for a reason that metal_profile=1 removes: with discovery on,
#     SpringBoard used to abort for ever in Siri's orb, because MPSDevice::GetMPSDevice asks
#     -supportsFamily: from Apple8 down to Apple2 and this device claimed Apple1 alone (10.47).
#     metal_profile=1 claims Apple2, and the boot of 01:18 had SpringBoard alive with it. So
#     THIS boot is the first that can have both a window server on Metal and an application on
#     it, and if SpringBoard aborts again that pairing is what the log is about.
/redwood/bin/metal_props on
#
# 2. The lock screen out of the way, the way rc.jobs.unlock does it, and time for SpringBoard
#    to have finished coming up. Forty seconds is what rc.jobs.launch waits for the same thing.
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 40 swipe 0.5 0.985 0.5 0.3 250 wait 6
#
# 3. THE APPLICATION, launched the way a link launches one: SpringBoard is asked to open the
#    bundle's own URL scheme (src/triangle_app.plist, CFBundleURLTypes -> redwoodtriangle).
#    This needs no icon to be tapped and no icon position to be guessed, and SpringBoard knows
#    the scheme because LaunchServices rebuilt its database by walking /Applications at boot.
#
#    IF THIS LINE SAYS "it REFUSES", the launch can still be done by hand and the run is not
#    wasted: the icon is a blue square with an orange triangle in its lower left corner -- the
#    same picture the application draws -- and tapping it gives exactly the same log lines.
/redwood/bin/battery_test open redwoodtriangle://
#
# 4. Who is running, from a program of Apple's that knows nothing about any of this. The list
#    must have a RedwoodTriangle in it; if it does not, SpringBoard took the URL and the
#    process died before exec, and the kernel's own lines above are where to look.
#
#    TAKE THE SCREENSHOT ANY TIME AFTER THIS LINE: ./xl screenshot logs/triangle.png
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 20
/bin/ps -A
#
# 5. THE DRAWABLE AGAIN, IN A PROCESS WITH NO WINDOW, and the ORDER HERE IS THE ONE DECISION IN
#    this list that is not obvious, so it is written down. The same binary in its --layer mode:
#    a bare CAMetalLayer of the panel's size, a device built by hand, Apple's clearFragment01,
#    one triangle, and the frame read back both ways. It answers questions 1 and 2 and nothing
#    about 3.
#
#    It would be natural to run it FIRST -- it costs four seconds and its output is CERTAIN to
#    be in the log, which an application's cannot be promised to be (it is started for
#    SpringBoard and nothing guarantees it the descriptors init gave launchd, which is why
#    src/triangle_app.m opens /dev/console by name, as src/inject_app.c does). It runs here
#    instead, and the reason is a measured one: ONE HUNG DISPATCH LATCHES THIS GPU and makes
#    every dispatch after it empty (docs/air.md, the AIR suite's canary). The one thing this
#    boot can produce that no log can is the PHOTOGRAPH, so the photograph gets the undisturbed
#    GPU and this process goes after it. If the application has already drawn, nothing is lost;
#    if it never launched, this still runs and still answers two of the three questions.
#
#    Two processes, two users -- uid 501 above and uid 0 here -- and the kernel makes one GPU
#    buffer per user client, so a result that held for only one of them would be about the
#    client and not about the surface. That is the same argument rc.jobs.drawable makes for its
#    second, smaller run.
#
#    It draws TWO frames. The first does everything except present; the second presents, last
#    of all and after every number is printed, because a present with no window server behind
#    it is the one call in this mode most likely to end the process, and it must not cost what
#    came before it.
#
#    AND ONE THING ABOUT THIS MODE THAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, said here rather than discovered:
#    whether -nextDrawable works at all on a layer with no superlayer and no window. It is
#    ordinary practice elsewhere and -[CAMetalLayer nextDrawable] (0x18829de60) does guard its
#    nil cases -- a nil device at 0x18829e07c branches away rather than faulting -- but it has
#    never been done here, and the CAImageQueue a drawable is registered in is the layer's. If
#    this line dies, the last "triangle: step:" line names the call, and nothing the
#    application already measured is lost, which is the other half of why it runs last.
/redwood/bin/triangle_app --layer
#
# 6. And then nothing but time, so that the application keeps repainting and the screenshot can
#    be taken again -- once while the application is alone on the GPU and once after step 5 has
#    also used it, which is worth having as two photographs rather than one.
/redwood/bin/rwtouch wait 180
/bin/ps -A
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# HOW TO READ THE LOG (it has NUL bytes in it, so every grep needs -a)
#
# AND FIRST, THE ONE THING THAT MAKES A LOG UNREADABLE RATHER THAN NEGATIVE. The user-space part
# of the log is a RING, and it is not in chronological order once it has wrapped. If this
# application's lines are missing ALTOGETHER -- not even "triangle: start:" -- check for a wrap
# before concluding anything: if lines stamped later in the boot stand ABOVE lines stamped
# earlier, or if the job echoes ("$ /redwood/bin/...") stop partway down, then the buffer
# wrapped and the absence means nothing. That is exactly what logs/055044-run.log is: built
# without QUIET=1, 1321 of its console lines are launchd's accounting from the single second
# 05:50:45, the echoes stop after the third job line, and not one line of this application
# survived -- though it demonstrably ran, because the kernel's own ring recorded its abort.
# Build with QUIET=1 and this does not arise.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle:' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       everything both processes said, in order. The whole measurement.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: start:' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       TWO lines, and the difference between them is the point: "mode --layer" as uid 0, and
#       "mode application" as uid 501. One line only, naming --layer, means the application
#       never ran -- go to the battery_test line's own output. Neither line means /dev/console could not be
#       opened by either, which has never happened and would be about init, not about Metal.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: FAILED' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       the only lines that must not be there.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: NOT MEASURED' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       the honest non-answers, each naming which: no plug-in bundle, no IOAcceleratorES
#       service, no shader library on the disk, no memory for a readback, or a drawable whose
#       IOSurface could not be reached. None of them is a failure of the path and none of them
#       may be read as one.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: .* of 2740500 pixels are not the colour' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       THE HEADLINE. Two lines a counted frame -- one "surface count", one "getBytes count" --
#       and "0 of 2740500" on both is the boot this file was written for. A number here is the
#       count of pixels that disagree with the rasterisation rule, and the line after it names
#       the first one and what it reads.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: surface count\|triangle: getBytes count' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       the tallies: orange, blue, NEITHER, and what the rule says. "1370250 orange and
#       1370250 blue" is the answer -- BUT SEE THE TRAP IN THE TABLE ABOVE: those two numbers
#       are the same for the right picture and for a picture flipped or turned, because the
#       areas are equal. What distinguishes them is the "not the colour the rule says" line
#       above and the named pixels below, not this one. Any pixel counted NEITHER is a colour
#       nothing in this program can produce, and then the first-wrong line says what it was.
#       AND THE ONE COMPARISON THAT MATTERS MOST: these two lines come from two different
#       views of the same memory at two different strides. If "surface" is right and
#       "getBytes" is wrong, or the other way round, the import has the pitch wrong; if both
#       are right and the panel is black, the texture was a copy.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: .* check: pixel' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       six named pixels a view, with what each must be and what it is. This is what says WHICH
#       WAY a wrong picture is wrong: (225,1948) and (900,487) are the two far from the edge,
#       and (506,1339) / (618,1096) straddle the edge eight hundredths of the panel apart.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: frame' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       one line every twentieth frame, plus every frame that went wrong. What this is for is
#       the frame NUMBER: if it stops at 2 or 3 and "nextDrawable gave NIL" follows, the pool
#       of drawables is full, which means THE PRESENTS ARE NOT COMPLETING -- the signature of
#       question 3 failing while 1 and 2 pass, and it cannot be mistaken for anything else.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: drawable:\|triangle: frame 1: drawable' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       what came back: the drawable's class, its texture's class, its size, its pixel format
#       and its usage. The usage must be 4 -- MTLTextureUsageRenderTarget alone, which is what
#       framebufferOnly = YES hard-codes (docs/metal.md 10.46) and what the compiler library
#       requires of a colour attachment.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: frame 1: its IOSurface' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       the surface the drawable is over: its id, its size and its stride. For 1125 x 2436
#       BGRA the stride must be 4544 (1125 x 4 = 4500, and MINISURF_ROW_ALIGN is 64). 4500
#       here would mean the kernel stopped aligning and the import should then be REFUSED by
#       name rather than succeed.
#
#   grep -a 'redwoodmetal: no drawable:' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       the driver's own refusals, which are the second reference for every nil above: the
#       plane, the surface id, the pixel format, the geometry disagreement, the Adreno pitch
#       rule, or a compiler library without rwa_texture_from_surface. Each names its number.
#       EMPTY is the answer.
#
#   grep -a 'redwooddrawable:' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       the bridge's own one-off lines, once a process: whether libredwoodair.dylib is loaded
#       here and whether it has rwa_texture_from_surface, and whether this process had
#       IOSurfaceGetID.
#
#   grep -a "this pipeline's vertex stage was generated" logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       must be there: it is the driver saying it supplied the quad stage, which is what makes
#       a draw of three vertices a triangle. Absent means a vertex function got in somehow and
#       the geometry above is not what was drawn.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: device:' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       WHICH ROAD THE DEVICE CAME BY, which is the first thing to read in this log now that
#       there are two. In order it must say:
#
#         "the registry switch is MetalPluginName RedwoodMetal, MetalPluginClassName
#          RedwoodMetalDevice, so Metal's own discovery WILL run" -- read out of the registry
#          before Metal is asked anything, and it must agree with what metal_props printed.
#          "(none)" here with metal=1 in the command line means metal_props failed.
#
#       and then exactly ONE of these two, never both:
#
#         "Metal's own discovery gave RedwoodMetalDevice, so this process adds NOTHING"
#              the discovery-on road, and the road a game takes. Nothing else of the device
#              section follows it: no bundle, no step 4, no step 5, no MTLAddDevice.
#         "MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice() = nil, so Metal has no device of its own here and
#          this process builds one"
#              the discovery-off road, and then the bundle/step 4/step 5/MTLAddDevice lines
#              follow and the last of them says "built in this process".
#
#       BOTH ROADS IN ONE PROCESS WOULD BE THE BUG OF logs/055044-run.log COMING BACK, and it
#       cannot be read off the log afterwards because the process would be dead -- see the next
#       grep, which is the one that catches it.
#
#   grep -a 'userabort: triangle_app\|userabort: RedwoodTriangle' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       must be EMPTY. If a frame of it unslides to MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice + 140
#       (0x181a34bc8), the process registered a second Metal device and Apple's own assertion
#       killed it -- "MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice doesn't know what to do with more than one
#       device" -- and that is this application's own mistake and not a finding about the port.
#       tools/metal/unslide.py reads such a stack.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: step:' logs/NNNNNN-run.log | tail -3
#       where it stopped, if it stopped.
#
#   grep -a 'RedwoodGPU: request' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       empty. A line here is the kernel being asked something it does not know, and 0x7e
#       (kRedwoodSurface) is the request the whole drawable depends on.
#
#   grep -a 'MiniIOSurface' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       NOT expected to be empty, and that is the point of grepping it. "selector 40 not
#       implemented" and "selector 41" are already known to appear on the CAMetalLayer path
#       (docs/metal.md 10.47 put them next to -setDrawableSize:); the kernel's own table stops
#       at 35 (MiniIOSurface.cpp), each unknown selector is refused with kIOReturnUnsupported,
#       and 10.47 established that those two are not fatal -- SpringBoard went past them to the
#       MPS assertion. So the two are the BASELINE here; a THIRD number, or a complaint about a
#       format or a plane count, is new and is about this drawable. Note that the printer
#       de-duplicates per boot and does not name the process, so neither the count of lines nor
#       the caller can be read off it.
#
#   grep -a 'MPSDevice\|failed assertion' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       must be EMPTY. "Failed to get MPSDevice" means something put the registry properties
#       on after all and this boot has no window server (SpringBoard aborts and launchd
#       restarts it for ever); the application's own numbers might still stand but the
#       screenshot would be worthless. The assertion is the aborting process's own standard
#       error, so it is in the log even under QUIET=1, which launchd's accounting is not.
#
#   grep -a 'triangle: done:' logs/NNNNNN-run.log
#       the totals, one a mode.
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT DOES NOT ANSWER, said here so that nothing in it is read as more than it is
#
#   - NOTHING IS TIMED. The 15.46 ms a frame costs today without a drawable (docs/metal.md
#     10.43) is not re-measured and the cost of a frame WITH one is not measured either. This
#     list says whether the path works, not what it costs; the arithmetic in 10.46 says which
#     part of the saving is subtraction and which part is a guess, and this boot moves neither.
#   - THE FRAME RATE IS NOT MEASURED AND IS NOT THE FRAME RATE. The application draws twice a
#     second deliberately, so that the picture is still and a screenshot can be taken whenever;
#     the half second is a timer, not a limit.
#   - NO SHADER OF OURS RUNS. The fragment stage is Apple's clearFragment01 and the vertex
#     stage is the compiler library's generated quad. Nothing here compiles a shader written for
#     this port, because there is no Metal compiler on the build machine -- and that is a
#     limitation of the toolchain, not of the driver.
#   - IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT A SECOND LAYER, A SECOND WINDOW OR A RESIZE. One layer, one size,
#     set once. A drawable whose size changes under it is a different measurement.
#   - IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT ANY APPLE APPLICATION. With the registry properties off, Settings
#     and Safari and SpringBoard itself use the same CoreAnimation path they used yesterday, and
#     nothing in this boot changes what they do. "Metal in a system application" needs the
#     properties on, and that needs the MPS assertion above dealt with first.
#   - IT DOES NOT SAY THE WINDOW SERVER COMPOSITED **OUR** SURFACE RATHER THAN A COPY OF IT.
#     The screenshot proves the pixels arrived; which copy of them arrived is not separated
#     here, and would need the window server's own side instrumented.
